  <result type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Outcome 1: Independent and resilient public interest media are more viable and produce and disseminate ethical, gender-inclusive, diverse content and provide fora for constructive public debate online and offline</narrative>
   </title>
   <reference code="275416" vocabulary="99"></reference>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="1" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Partners increase their ability to promote citizen engagement with and access to high-quality, fact-based coverage about issues of public interest, including corruption. 
- Targeted media are more viable and better equipped to produce high quality content and engage audiences
- Examples of audiences utilising media content to participate in public debate % audience increased</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-12-31"></period-end>
     <target></target>
     <actual value="- 25 media partners (20 in Ukraine and 5 in Georgia) have strengthened their ability to produce engaging high-quality content of public interest. 5 media partners have worked to counter gendered disinformation and strengthen DEI in the Georgian media landscape. Fostering lasting collaboration beyond traditional development cooperation, 9 Danish, Georgian, and Ukrainian media outlets strengthened transnational ties between Denmark and the ENC through twinning activities
- 14 media partners (9 in Ukraine and 5 in Georgia) have increased their media viability; now better equipped to produce high quality content and engage audiences
- 20 investigations (13 in Ukraine and 7 in Georgia) led to concrete action to strengthen accountability e.g. strengthened transparency in public procurement systems in Ukraine and governmental action on environmental issues in Georgia
- All media partners increased their audience reach, for example Hromadske increased their monthly audience from 6 million (2022 data) to 23 million"></actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target></target>
     <actual value="Only reporting in final year"></actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-12-31"></period-end>
     <target></target>
     <actual value="Only reporting in final year"></actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Outcome 3: Media sector utilises technological solutions for strengthening freedom of expression and furthering accountability of authorities and tech companies</narrative>
   </title>
   <reference code="280764" vocabulary="99"></reference>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Media stakeholders take action in favour of accountability and the use of technological solutions.</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-12-31"></period-end>
     <target></target>
     <actual value="- 33 stakeholders (7 in GE /25 in Ukraine) take action in favour of accountability and the use of technological solutions
- 5 organisations formed the Ukrainian National Tech coalition fostering dialogue between Ukrainian civil society and Big Tech (Microsoft, Google/YouTube, Meta, Twitter, and later TikTok) following 9 War and Disinformation roundtables.
- 11 key media organisations take part in the Consortium to oversee the establishment of a press card system/self- regulatory mechanism. The Memorandum and Regulations for the governing body are developed and approved
- In Georgia, MDFs monitoring of gendered disinformation has informed public interest content and strengthened 16 journalists capacity in gender-sensitive reporting
- Five media partners in Georgia have established a Rapid Response Group (RRG) based on a data-driven pre-bunking; this model was cascaded to Georgia from the Rapid Response Pilot Group in Moldova (under M-MIIND). The RRG was launched in September 2024, and is informed by AI monitoring of disinformation trends in the Georgian information space."></actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target></target>
     <actual value="Only reporting in final year"></actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-12-31"></period-end>
     <target></target>
     <actual value="Only reporting in final year"></actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Outcome 2: Local safety support is available to protect media workers of all genders and mitigate the risks they face</narrative>
   </title>
   <reference code="280763" vocabulary="99"></reference>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">% of partners and journalists confirm the relevance and significance of local safety support.</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-12-31"></period-end>
     <target></target>
     <actual value="90-95% of media and journalists confirm the relevance of and need for safety support in Ukraine. In Georgia, safety needs identified, training model developed to strengthen legal professionals. Support (legal and financial) provided to partners to mitigate the government&apos;s repressive measures."></actual>
